WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:06.733
- The Bank of England is issuing new bank notes,
beginning with the £5 note.

00:00:06.733 --> 00:00:13.067
And they're made of plastic, and there've been
all sorts of advertisements that you can't break them.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:13.980
I felt immediately: challenged

00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:20.133
and I had the idea,
if we froze it with liquid nitrogen...

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.967
the strands of the polymer
would be frozen rigid...

00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:34.433
and you may be able to break it—
hitting it with a hammer.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:41.467
So I gave Neil several of my own £5 notes—

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.900
you can't spend university
money on things like this

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.633
—and he had great pleasure trying
to break the professor's bank notes.

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:55.067
And it worked!

00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:59.700
He managed to smash one,
but it took several attempts.

00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:05.267
You had to get the orientation
just right and hit it just in the right way,

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.767
and then it broke into two or three pieces.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:14.267
Unfortunately, he lost one of the pieces,
so I couldn't glue it together and spend the note,

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:15.700
so it's a souvenir.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:21.533
Incidentally, it's not illegal to break the
notes, though it's illegal to draw pictures

00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:23.733
on her Majesty's face.

00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:29.933
But Neil felt that this wasn't
really quite exciting enough,

00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:35.967
so he decided to put this in his
special supply of fuming nitric acid.

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:40.467
It's a bit old, so it's gone brown,
so it may be even better.

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:47.067
Fuming nitric acid... and there's several sorts,
and I'm not sure what Neil's type was particularly,

00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:53.400
but essentially, it is a mixture of
pure nitric acid—or nearly pure nitric acid

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:59.067
with N2O4 dinitrogen tetroxide added to it.

00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:06.433
And N2O4 in itself can dissociate into NO2,
so it's quite a witch's brew

00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:09.967
or perhaps I should say
wizard's brew in the case of Neil.

00:02:09.967 --> 00:02:14.933
It is well know as being
a very powerful cleaning agent.

00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:21.800
If you can't get muck off your glassware,
the last resort is fuming nitric acid.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.900
Neil's hypothesis was that he could
completely dissolve it—it would just disappear

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.200
and he could have a solution of a £5 note.

00:02:31.333 --> 00:02:34.967
What happened was actually much more exciting,

00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:41.033
that when he put it in,
the queen's face quickly faded.

00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:47.767
When he took it out, there was some
of the writing and stuff still left.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:58.867
But it just brushed off quite easily
and what he was left with, to all our surprise,

00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:06.300
was a completely transparent piece of plastic,
exactly the same size that he started with.

00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:10.267
- yeah, lovely.

00:03:10.333 --> 00:03:15.733
- I gave this sample to one of my
student who ran an infrared spectrum

00:03:15.733 --> 00:03:18.433
and showed that it was polypropylene.

00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:21.133
This is the plastic made from propyne.

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:25.600
When it says on the internet these
are made from polypropylene, they're right!

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.400
It's really fun. I now show this to
people and say "what do you think it is"

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.000
and they have no idea.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.900
You can see that we did a proper
experiment in the sense that we had a control.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:42.733
I insisted that they also use
one of the old paper notes.

00:03:42.733 --> 00:03:49.400
The queen didn't fade as much on the paper note,
but when they had taken it out and dried it,

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:55.000
it was interesting that the note had
gained really quite a lot of weight.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.600
And this was absolutely consistent with my idea

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:04.667
that the fuming nitric acid might
nitrate the cellulose of the paper.

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:09.000
This is adding NO2 groups to the paper,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.633
and nitrated paper burns very
much better than ordinary paper.

00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:28.500
It's used by magicians and conjurers
and it's the basis, in fact, of gun cotton,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.900
which is used as an explosive.

00:04:37.533 --> 00:04:39.733
It went with a real woosh!

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:45.967
And what was particularly interesting
is that it burned without any ash at all.

00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:52.433
It just disappeared, and the reason
for this is that with the NO2 groups,

00:04:52.433 --> 00:04:58.967
you have enough oxygen in the
note to make it burn to completion.

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:06.733
So we have a really nice
farewell for the old paper bank note.

00:05:06.733 --> 00:05:09.567
It just disappears into thin air.

00:05:12.667 --> 00:05:18.300
I must stress that we've done this in the
interest of science and because we think

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.733
it's an important and interesting experiment.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.000
But, on the other hand, we are destroying
bank notes, and so we feel that it's only right

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.700
that we've given an
equal amount of money to charity

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:36.767
to show that we don't really want to
destroy money just for the sake of it.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.333
I should say to you that if you
want to repeat these experiments,

00:05:42.333 --> 00:05:46.200
there are probably cheaper ways
of doing it than using bank notes.

